==English==

===Etymology===
The phrase, from Scotland, originally meant to ask a guy out if you yourself was a man. "can i pull your leg?" ultimately meaning can I suck your [penis]. 

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-pull someone's leg.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[tease]] someone; to lead someone on; to [[goad]] someone into overreacting. It usually implies teasing or goading by [[jokingly]] [[lying]].
#* '''1934''', [[w:Rex Stout|Rex Stout]], [[w:Fer-de-Lance (book)|''Fer-de-Lance'']], 1992 [[w:Bantam Books|Bantam]] edition, {{ISBN|0553278193}}, page 111:
#*: I hadn't '''pulled Mrs. Barstow's leg''' for any of that stuff, she had just handed it to me on a platter, and that wasn't my fault.
#* {{quote-av
|en
|actor=Eric Idle
|role=Mr. Cheeky
|title=Life of Brian
|year=1979
|url=https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0079470/quotes/qt0471957
|passage= No, I'm just '''pulling your leg''', it's crucifixion really.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|obsolete}} To [[extract]] [[money]] from someone (by taking out a [[loan]] or by [[swindling]]). {{defdate|late 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|title=An Old-Time Tip|author=Mark Well|journal=Wallace's Monthly|volume=12
|url=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Wallace_s_Monthly/XkoCAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22pull+his+leg%22&pg=PA497
|text=I'm very glad to hear of his good fortune; but it's a deuced strange thing, for I saw him at the club last night and he never said a word of it. I'll '''pull his leg''' when I see him.
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Coffee and Repartee|year=1893|author=John Kendrick Bangs
|url=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/The_Ridpath_Library_of_Universal_Literat/1L0qAQAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22pull+his+leg%22&pg=PA394
|text=The verb ''''to-pull-his-leg'''' means to extract from his pocket all the lucre it will yield.
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|title=Reports of Decisions in Probate
|url=https://archive.org/details/reportsdecision01coffgoog/page/n195/
|text="I would ''''pull his leg''',' but Laura will not work with me." The expression "'''pulling his leg'''," Miss Sullivan explained as meaning to coax or wheedle his wealth out of him.
}} <!-- NB: Book of decisions was published 1909, but it appears this particular decision dates to 1895. -->

